Tewari et al. (Ref  31 ) observed by TEM and SAXS that in the early stages of precipitation at low temperatures (from 400 to 450 °C), phases such as  μ  (C14 structure), S (hexagonal), and X (hexagonal) are formed. On the other hand, at higher temperatures (from 450 to 500 °C) and longer times, martensite reverts to austenite, associated with the precipitation of phases Ni 3 (Ti, Mo) and Laves (Fe 2 Mo). In maraging 300 steel without titanium, the  ω  phase can be formed from phases generated early in the aging process. The  ω  phase is observed in metallic alloys containing elements of group 4 of the periodic table (e.g., Ti, Zr, and Hf), particularly when the  β  phase (body-centered cubic), which occurs at high temperatures, is stabilized relative to the  α  phase (hexagonal). In such alloys, the formation of the athermal ω phase occurs through a displacive transformation, characterized by coordinated, wave-like atomic displacements within the body-centered cubic lattice without long-range diffusion and with minimal change in transformation enthalpy. In contrast, the formation of the ordered  ω  phase follows a mixed transformation pathway, in which displacive transformations may occur simultaneously or sequentially with thermally activated reconstructive or diffusional processes that involve atomic rearrangements over distances exceeding the interatomic spacing. For clarity, Fig.  2  presents a time–temperature–transformation (TTT) diagram, while Table  2  summarizes the main phases reported in maraging steels. Fig. 2 Generic TTT diagram illustrating precipitation reactions in maraging steels (Adapted from Ref  31 ) Table 2 Phases commonly observed in maraging steels, summarizing their stoichiometry, crystal structure, lattice parameters, and crystallographic orientation relationships with austenite or martensite (Ref  31 ) Phase Stoichiometry Structure Cell parameters Orientation relationship Austenite (A) … FCC a  = 3.5852 Å {110} M //{111} A Martensite (M) … 