Vasudevan, Kim, and Wayman (Ref  13 ) investigated precipitation kinetics and hardening mechanisms in two maraging 250 steels: C-250 (18Ni–8-9Co–4-5Mo–0.5Ti wt.%) and T-250 (18Ni– ~ 1.5Ti, nearly Co-free). Differences in chemical composition resulted in distinct types and distributions of strengthening precipitates. Both alloys were aged at 427, 482, and 538 °C for times ranging from 0.017 to 100 h. Precipitation kinetics was evaluated using electrical resistivity measurements fitted to the Kolmogorov–Johnson–Mehl–Avrami (KJMA) equation, and activation energies were determined using the Arrhenius relationship. The relatively low activation energy for precipitation (~ 130 kJ·mol −1 ) was attributed to the preferential nucleation of precipitates at dislocations within martensite blocks (Fig.  4 a). Fig. 4 (a) Nucleation of precipitates at dislocations within martensite blocks in T-250 steel aged at 482 °C for 1 h; (b) dark-field TEM image obtained from the ( 22 4 ¯ 0 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
