Chandra and Gaur’s study (Ref  79 ) investigated the high-cycle fatigue behavior of PBF-LB fabricated 18Ni maraging steel using three specimen sizes with gauge volumes of 128 mm 3  (base size), 250 mm 3  (scaled by 25%), and 432 mm 3  (scaled by 50%) under fully reversed loading conditions ( R  = −1). By combining mechanical testing with a probabilistic defect-based framework, the authors assessed whether specimen size influences fatigue response and to what extent manufacturing defects control crack initiation. The microstructure and pore distributions were found to be similar among all specimen sizes, which explains the negligible differences in tensile and bulk fatigue properties. Notably, most defects were located within the subsurface region, approximately 400 μm below the free surface, rather than being uniformly distributed throughout the volume, and this defect distribution dictated the scatter in fatigue life. Figure  15  compares four fatigue-life prediction models: the Basquin model, the Shimatani model, the  Z -parameter model, and the  X -parameter model. The Basquin approach, which does not account for defect size or location, showed a weak correlation with the experimental data and a large scatter band for all specimen sizes, reflecting its inability to capture the defect-controlled fatigue behavior inherent to PBF-LB materials. On the other hand, the Shimatani model, which integrates the  area \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
